---
/**
 * patterns/band — the theme's centred band, with whatever the page puts in it.
 *
 * `/orbiters` holds four of these and its Spanish half holds the same four:
 * eight copies of two nested elements that carry no words of their own. What
 * goes inside differs every time — a line of prose, a heading, a heading with a
 * picture and a sentence — so the content is a slot and the component is only
 * the band. MW-19.
 *
 * ── THE CLASS NAMES ARE THE THEME'S, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ──────────────────
 *
 * `hero hero--center` and `hero__content` belong to the dead Jekyll theme, and
 * `legacy.css` still draws them — scoped `.prose`, where article bodies render.
 * Rewriting them under honest names would be a second spelling of a live rule,
 * which is the reasoning `media/EmbedGroup` and `media/EmbedPlate` both record.
 * When `legacy.css` goes, this file changes once instead of eight bodies.
 *
 * ── `hero--dark` IS NOT CARRIED, AND NOTHING IS LOST ─────────────────────────
 *
 * Two of the four bands had it. On this site it draws nothing: `.prose .hero`
 * already sets `padding-block: var(--s-8)`, and `.prose .hero--dark` sets the
 * same padding, `background: none` and `border-radius: var(--r-0)` — every one
 * of which is what the element does without it. It was a modifier for a LIGHT
 * page, and MW-16 removed the light surface. Two of the four bands having it is
 * a difference nobody chose and nobody can see.
 *
 * ── AND NEITHER IS `style="background-color: #000000"` ───────────────────────
 *
 * All four carried it, in three spellings (`#000000` twice, `#000` twice).
 * `--sf-base` IS `#000000`, so on screen it painted black onto black. It was not
 * harmless, though: `tokens.css` swaps `--sf-base` to the cream `--sf-paper`
 * under `@media print`, and an inline background ignores that — so printing
 * `/orbiters` laid four black bands across a cream page. Dropping it fixes the
 * printed page and changes nothing on screen.
 */
---

<div class="hero hero--center">
  <div class="hero__content">
    <slot />
  </div>
</div>
